sure there are some arithmetic operators  used for performing calculations in a s  when you use more than one arithmetic  operator in an expression operations of  priority one are performed before  operations of priority 2 and so on  consecutive operations that have the  same priority are performed from right  to left with in priority one from left  to right within priorities 2 and 3 in  the next screen we will have a look at  logical operators used in SAS  sure here are some logical operators  used to compare to SAS values the result  of logical comparison in SAS is in a  true or false format in the next few  screens we will observe how to use  logical SAS expressions with conditional  statements to control the flow of  program execution of any program can be  controlled using conditional statements  upon execution of these statements the  prog compiler decides the set of  statements to be executed and the set to  be ignored SAS offers different  conditional statements to select the set  of observations to be processed in  program execution the first one in this  series is the where statement this  statement can be used to select  observations during the PR OC step and  data step the syntax for using the where  statement is as shown here the where  expression specifies a condition for  selecting observations it can be any  valid SAS expression the where statement  works for both character and numeric  variables to specify a condition based  on the value of a character variable  enclose the value in quotation marks  considering that the comparison will be  case sensitive in the next screen we  will show an example to understand how  the where statement works in a full one  only those observations from dataset  clinic dot admit with an age greater  than 30 will be processed in the next  example we combine two comparisons using  the or operator here only those  observations from dataset clinic dot  admit which have act level either low or  mod will be processed next we will  discuss the if-then-else condition  operator the f  then statement executes an SAS statement  when the condition in the if Clause is  true any comparison and logical  operators can be used in the if  conditional expression any numeric value  other than zero or missing is true and a  value of zero or missing is false the  syntax for using this is as shown if the  condition in the if Clause is evaluated  to be true then statement set one is  executed if the condition in the if  Clause is evaluated to be false and  condition in the if Clause is evaluated  to be true then statement set to will be  executed in case the expressions in if  and else if are evaluated to false then  only statement set 3 will be executed in  the next screen we will take an example  to understand the flow of the if then  else statement
 

